 1 | security-reviewer | CHARTER OVERRIDE, FIRST AND BINDING: read-only reviewer. No commits, no push, no gh pr review, no gh pr merge, no labels, no test writing. NEVER execute infra/monitoring/orphan-alerts.sh: it creates real GCP resources. Permitted writes are a /tmp worktree only. SCOPE: a security pass across four PRs, before synthesis. Record each PR head SHA at start. PR 2717: a scoped eslint-disable in apps/web/lib/session-expiry.ts keeps a full document reload on session expiry, and the author claims that reload is what actually invalidates the client session, by re-running a mount-only hydration at apps/web/lib/auth-context.tsx line 193 and dropping in-flight requests still carrying an expired Bearer token. Verify that as a SECURITY property, not a lint preference: does anything else clear the token, can an in-flight request still succeed with it, and is the destination genuinely internal with an encoded parameter so there is no open redirect. PR 2712: confirm no secret, webhook URL, notification-channel email address or project-identifying payload is committed into the repo by this PR. PR 2716: confirm a hermes vulnerability finding actually FAILS the matrix aggregate rather than merely reporting it, and that the nightly tracker still files on the schedule event, so the gate blocks rather than informs. PR 2719: confirm guest legal-link reachability survives, since the mobile menu now carries links that the Art. 5 contract in issue 1586 requires to stay one interaction away. Post ONE comment on issue 2340 whose first line reads CRITIC SECURITY PR 2712 2716 2717 2719, with a per-PR verdict of SAFE-TO-MERGE or BLOCK-FIX or BLOCK-ESCALATE plus file:line. No emails, booking ids, gcloud payloads or vendor names in that comment. | review/security-sweep-2026-08-19
 2 | investigate | CHARTER OVERRIDE, FIRST AND BINDING: read-only reviewer, not a producer and not a devops operator. No commits, no push, no gh pr review, no gh pr merge, no label changes. NEVER run terraform apply, NEVER run terraform plan against live state, and NEVER execute infra/monitoring/orphan-alerts.sh: it creates real GCP resources. Permitted writes are a /tmp worktree only. SCOPE: PR 2712, live-versus-code drift ONLY; runbook prose is the next seat, do not assess it. During incident 2711 three monitoring resources were applied by hand in project olympus-ai-tech: a log-based metric named duffel_balance_insufficient, an email notification channel, and an alert policy titled Duffel Balance is empty. This PR codifies them into infra/monitoring/orphan-alerts.tf and orphan-alerts.sh. Do exactly two things. First, verify the codified definitions MATCH the live resources, using read-only gcloud describe and list calls only, diffing field by field, and report any drift precisely. If credentials or the project are unavailable, report BLOCKED-PRECONDITION and stop, and do NOT substitute reading the file for querying live state. Second, verify the extended parity gate in scripts/check-alert-policy-parity.py cannot pass while the tf and sh files disagree, by drifting one field IN A THROWAWAY COPY, confirming it fails, then confirming the real files pass. Post ONE comment on issue 2340 whose first line reads CRITIC PR 2712 DRIFT, with a verdict of SAFE-TO-MERGE or BLOCK-FIX or BLOCK-ESCALATE or BLOCKED-PRECONDITION. Never include a channel email address or a gcloud payload in that comment or in any file left in the repo. | ops/duffel-balance-alert
 3 | backend-critic | CHARTER OVERRIDE, FIRST AND BINDING: read-only reviewer. No commits, no push, no gh pr review, no gh pr merge, no labels, no test writing, and never execute infra/monitoring/orphan-alerts.sh. Permitted writes are a /tmp worktree only. SCOPE: PR 2712, BEHAVIOUR CLAIMS ONLY; the live drift check is a separate seat, do not repeat it. This PR rewrites docs/operations/duffel-balance-funding.md, and the rewrite asserts things about code. Prove or refute each against source. One: the previously documented Cloud Logging query filtered on jsonPayload.alert and jsonPayload.error, neither of which exists on the handler warning line, so it returned zero rows during the very outage it exists for. Two: the fields the corrected query filters on DO exist on the emitted log line; leave live row counts to the drift seat. Three: the old doc claimed the code cancels the Stripe authorisation and marks the booking failed, and it does neither on a 422, because service/booking.go skips voiding for recoverable errors and the row stays pending, so a traveller can hold a live authorisation for roughly seven days while the reaper worker is paused; walk the client retry path too, not only the handler. Four: the rewritten orphan-alerts.tf header is honest that everything EXCEPT the balance alert remains unapplied. CRITICAL COUPLING: PR 2714 changes the 422, hold and retry behaviour this runbook documents. Read 2714 at head d32c7f09 and state explicitly whether merging 2712 before 2714 would ship a runbook that describes behaviour the other PR has already changed. Record the head SHA at start and re-check at report. Post ONE comment on issue 2340 whose first line reads CRITIC PR 2712 CLAIMS, with a verdict plus file:line. A diff touching no money-path file can still change money behaviour: follow behaviour to the client, not the file list. | ops/duffel-balance-alert
 4 | frontend-critic | CHARTER OVERRIDE, FIRST AND BINDING: read-only reviewer. No commits, no push, no gh pr review, no gh pr merge, no labels, no test writing. The harness pushes the branch it checks out, so any commit on a producer ref is a production incident. Permitted writes are a /tmp worktree only. SCOPE: PR 2719, which restores navigation below 720px and adds the first 375px E2E coverage in the suite. Verify the fail-first fails on the INVARIANT, namely that Plan, Explore, Stays and Stories are UNREACHABLE at 375px on origin/main; a test failing only on a missing test id proves nothing. Reproduce it yourself, then confirm on the branch that those four are reachable by real interaction rather than merely present in the DOM. Verify accessibility is real: keyboard reachable, correct aria-expanded and aria-controls, focus behaviour matching the existing header pattern, axe still passing. Rule out three regressions: guest legal links must remain one interaction away per the Art. 5 contract in issue 1586, signed-in legal links must still live on the avatar menu, and the desktop header must not shift. The author reports a self-caught 10px desktop shift on every page, caused by the toggle wrapper still earning its flex gap when only the button was hidden, fixed by moving the breakpoint rule to the wrapper: verify that fix holds by diffing the rendered desktop header against origin/main. The author declined to mount the dead component at apps/web/components/site-header.tsx, citing a nested header element, a v1 Tailwind surface where PRD section 2 mandates a ra- port, wrong nav destinations, and a 768px breakpoint against the 720px where ra-nav actually collapses: judge whether that refusal was justified. New strings must be in docs/prd/02-shells.md or marked PROPOSED. Flag for human sign-off that guests lose the help trigger below 720px. Record the head SHA at start and re-check at report. Post ONE comment on issue 2340 whose first line reads CRITIC PR 2719, with a verdict plus file:line. | fix/2690-mobile-nav
 5 | backend-critic | CHARTER OVERRIDE, FIRST AND BINDING: read-only reviewer. No commits, no push, no gh pr review, no gh pr merge, no labels, no test writing. Permitted writes are a /tmp worktree only. SCOPE: PR 2716, which extends the go-vulncheck job in .github/workflows/ci-deep.yml to a matrix over apps/api and services/hermes. Required proofs, none inferred from YAML. One: the hermes leg ACTUALLY RAN and scanned hermes, cited from the workflow run log. Two: setup-go resolves each module toolchain from that module go-version-file rather than a hardcoded version. Three: go-vulncheck remains in the open-failure-issue needs list, so a nightly failure still files the tracker. Four: fail-fast is false, so an apps/api finding cannot abort the run before hermes is scanned, since that masking is the blind spot this change exists to close. Five: the issue-filing step stays gated on the schedule event, so a manual dispatch neither files spurious trackers nor blocks merges. Run make check-ci-wiring ONLY to confirm this PR did not break the ci.yml gate pair, and state plainly in your report that the checker hardcodes ci.yml and therefore cannot see ci-deep.yml at all. Say whether the absence of any mechanical wiring gate on ci-deep.yml deserves its own issue. Confirm no apps or services source was touched. Record the head SHA at start and re-check at report. Post ONE comment on issue 2340 whose first line reads CRITIC PR 2716, with a verdict plus file:line. | ci/hermes-vulncheck
 6 | frontend-critic | CHARTER OVERRIDE, FIRST AND BINDING: read-only reviewer. No commits, no push, no gh pr review, no gh pr merge, no labels, no test writing. Permitted writes are a /tmp worktree only. SCOPE: PR 2717. Allowed file scope is exactly three things, apps/web/package.json, the root package-lock.json, and a scoped eslint-disable in apps/web/lib/session-expiry.ts; flag any edit outside that. The PR moves eslint-config-next and the Next eslint plugin to 16.3.1 while keeping eslint-plugin-react-hooks pinned at 7.0.1. Judge the suppression ON THE MERITS rather than assuming the router is always right; the security half of that claim is a separate seat, so focus on correctness and maintainability. Confirm eslint-plugin-react-hooks is genuinely still 7.0.1 in the root lockfile and on disk, that scripts/check-react-placement.sh passes so React hoisting is undisturbed, and that no component effect logic was edited since that is Part B and assigned to a human. Do NOT require a Closes keyword: Refs is correct because Part B keeps issue 2678 open. Ask whether any test pins the suppression rationale, and say so if none does. If the suppression is wrong and the router is correct here, say so plainly. Record the head SHA at start and re-check at report. Post ONE comment on issue 2340 whose first line reads CRITIC PR 2717, with a verdict plus file:line. | fix/2678-part-a
 7 | cto | CHARTER OVERRIDE, FIRST AND BINDING: read-only synthesis. No commits, no push, no gh pr review, no gh pr merge, no labels, no delegation, no rebase, no merging of anything, and no Closes keyword anywhere. Permitted writes are a /tmp worktree only. SCOPE: produce ONE merge-order table across PR 2712, 2714, 2716, 2717, 2718 and 2719. Gather verdicts from two sources: handoff stubs from this dispatch if present, and comments on issue 2340 posted after 2026-08-18T20:00Z whose first line names a PR number, not only those beginning with CRITIC. Note that PR 2718 was reviewed at 2026-08-18T22:28 with no blocking findings and deliberately has no seat in this dispatch; carry that verdict forward rather than marking it unreviewed. For any PR with no such comment, run gh pr view with files and statusCheckRollup and mark it UNREVIEWED. Where two seats reviewed one PR, worse verdict wins: that is 2712 from seats 3, 4 and 2, and 2717 from seats 7 and 2. A BLOCKED-PRECONDITION from seat 3 means claims reviewed and drift unverified, which is NOT flat UNREVIEWED. For each PR give the verdict, blocking findings, the deploy surface, and a recommended merge position with the reason. Deploy surfaces: 2712 is docs and infra with no deploy, 2716 is CI only, 2714 triggers an API deploy, and 2717, 2718 and 2719 are web. Never recommend merging an UNREVIEWED PR or one whose head SHA moved mid-review. State the one real coupling: 2712 documents the 422 hold and retry behaviour that 2714 changes, so 2712 must not be recommended ahead of 2714 unless a seat confirmed the runbook still matches. For every blocked PR add a short dispatch-ready fix list. Post the table as ONE comment on issue 2340 whose first line reads CRITIC SYNTHESIS MERGE ORDER. Note that PR 2714 was reviewed in wave 1 of the preceding dispatch and returned BLOCK-FIX, so carry that verdict forward and do not mark it unreviewed. Recommend, never merge. Close with the note that further implementation goes through dispatch rather than chat. | review/synthesis-2026-08-19
